# A nominal subtype of a primitive is a `K_SUB` at run time and its payload is
# a pointer to a `KSub`. `k_is_heap` did not say so until 2026-08-30, and
# `k_cohort_pop` reads that predicate to decide whether a beat's result must be
# carried out of the arena before the rewind. So a subtype returned across the
# entry was taken for a scalar: the arena went back to the mark under it and
# the caller kept a dangling pointer, which read back as whatever the arena was
# reused for.
#
# The three ingredients are all here and all needed. The value has to be MADE
# in one call and STORED by another, both written in the entry beside it, so
# `k_cohort_pop` sees it cross; and it has to go into a container, because a
# value rendered on the spot is read before the arena is reused.
#
# WHICH of the six lines comes back wrong depends on what the arena held, so
# the pin is the whole output rather than any one line. On the parent commit
# the first is `6.898943556335e-310` where it should be `3` — a `KSub*`
# rendered as an f64, which is the shape that named the cause. Five lines
# reading correctly there is not the bug being narrow; it is a use-after-free
# reading memory that happened to still hold the right thing.
#
# `float64` was the parent that survived every arrangement, and that is
# structural: a float payload is the double itself, so there is nothing to
# dangle.
import "std/list"

type tick int

type note string

type gauge float64

type chest
  held

pub fn a_tally n
  tick n

pub fn a_label s
  note s

pub fn a_measure x
  gauge x

pub fn in_a_field v
  c = chest v
  "{c.held}"

pub fn in_a_list v
  xs = [v]
  "{list/to_list xs}"
